<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henan is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on the middle and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> as its capital. Often called the birthplace of Chinese civilisation, it holds the Erlitou site, the Shang capitals at Zhengzhou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>, and the old imperial capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. Many states of the Zhou period lay within it, and it remains one of China's most populous provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henan.jpg" alt="Henan" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_(for_divination).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 422–423 Emperor Mingyuan took Luoyang, Xuchang and Hulao and most of <b>Henan</b> and western Shandong; Emperor Wen's great invasion of 450 was lured across the Yellow River, flanked and destroyed, and Wei armies laid waste the provinces to the south before withdrawing in January 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>King Pan Geng is credited with moving the capital to Yin, near modern Anyang in <b>Henan</b>, around 1300 BCE, and the inscriptions found there, which begin in the reign of Wu Ding in the thirteenth century BCE, make the late Shang the first period of Chinese history documented…</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its remnants scattered to western Sichuan, northern Tibet and to <b>Henan</b> and Hebei, where they persisted into the middle of the Ming.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Erlitou culture was an early Bronze Age society and archaeological culture of the Yellow River valley, named after the site of Erlitou in Yanshi, <b>Henan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2021 study found that millet farmers from <b>Henan</b> and Shandong helped to form the Hongshan people, moving north by inland and coastal routes, and a 2025 study modelled them as a mixture of local Ancient Northeast Asian ancestry, probably from the Zhaobaogou culture, and that of Dawenkou-related…</blockquote><a href="/culture/hongshan-culture">Hongshan culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Excavations in the 1950s at Shanxian in western <b>Henan</b> identified a Miaodigou II phase (3000–2600 BCE), transitional between the earlier Yangshao culture and the Henan Longshan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/longshan-culture">Longshan culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
